#
# The jobs run.sh makes of the suites, and how long each takes: run.sh starts
# the longest first.  A suite not listed is one job, started last.
#
#   suite   job ("-" when the suite is one)   seconds   environment
#
# A suite that runs its tests under the planner and under ORCA is one job a
# pass, and diskquota's, pax's and resgroup's a job a group of their tests
# too, resgroup's each on a cgroup parent of its own (resgroup/run.sh).
# ALONE=1 jobs run after the rest, side by side with each other alone:
# resgroup's test that measures a group's share of the CPU, each pass on
# half the cores (CPUS).  The seconds are a run's, on the machine the port is
# built on (16 cores, 32 threads), with the jobs started as the memory let
# them, ten at once at most; they order the jobs and nothing else, and
# run.sh's summary gives each run's own.
#
# MEM= is the most memory the job holds, in megabytes -- its servers' data,
# WAL and shared memory, /tmp being a tmpfs -- as run.sh's summary gave it,
# and some to spare: run.sh starts a job only while there is that much left
# for it.  More than the summary said for the jobs the run's OOM killer
# stopped short (greenplum's, diskquota's regress, pax's toast, the ORCA
# pass of postgis_regress), and for resgroup's, whose servers move to cgroup
# parents outside the job's cgroup, where the summary does not see them.

resgroup               orca_cpu 103   PASSES=orca RG_GROUPS=cpu ALONE=1 CPUS=16-31 MEM=1500
resgroup               planner_cpu 102 PASSES=planner RG_GROUPS=cpu ALONE=1 CPUS=0-15 MEM=1500
resgroup               orca_cpuset 79 PASSES=orca RG_GROUPS=cpuset MEM=1500
resgroup               planner_cpuset 77 PASSES=planner RG_GROUPS=cpuset MEM=1500
resgroup               orca     144   PASSES=orca RG_GROUPS=main MEM=1500
resgroup               planner  127   PASSES=planner RG_GROUPS=main MEM=1500
